#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088817} :activity/id #uuid "115bfa9d-34ae-51ca-aa04-b0f23cf266a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088859 :tutorial/content "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x).\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.** \n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x).$ The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2.\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y\\cdot {x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+C \\\\ y=x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {x}^{2}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next." :tutorial/id #uuid "1066a4d7-d790-5316-9925-1ac0e928ce01" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14550 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2aeafed5-8ce5-5873-95fe-baefd4b2fe7b" :step/math-academy-id 597598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088818}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088860 :tutorial/content "Given a first-order linear ODE, we can find an integrating factor and use it to solve the equation using the following procedure.\n\n- **Step 1**: Write the equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\,.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Multiply both sides of the equation found in step 1 by $I(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nI(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + I(x)P(x)y = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 4**: Rewrite the left side as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr)\n$$\n\nIf steps 1 through 3 are done correctly, then we can always do this, and we can check that it's correct by differentiating. Our new equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr) = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 5**: Integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$ and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yI(x) & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{I(x)}(\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might be wondering where the formula for $I(x)$ comes from; we'll discuss this at the end of the lesson. First, let's apply this method to a concrete example in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "a6a381ed-e9ab-5deb-9d74-30900610edf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14551 :tutorial/title "The Integrating Factor Method"} :step/id #uuid "552b03ae-2839-592c-a5fa-667890639337" :step/math-academy-id 597599 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087452 :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671116} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671106}] :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} :step/id #uuid "0c98b113-16b4-5f8c-8b8c-a5ffbbbd075d" :step/math-academy-id 597600 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578007} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578005}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} :step/id #uuid "6c426f6e-80e3-59e6-8472-9394bf844946" :step/math-academy-id 597603 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087454 :question/id #uuid "09d09b0b-2132-59b2-9eb9-ae0748f09252" :question/math-academy-id "e-21743" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 3x^2$ and $Q(x) = 9x^2.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x) = e^{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 9{x}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot y & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}}) & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{{x}^{3}}+C \\\\ y & =3+C{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3+C{e}^{-{0}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048245}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495496} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495481}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"} :step/id #uuid "8f99a521-6d0e-5370-b62d-331c98f13937" :step/math-academy-id 597607 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088861 :tutorial/content "We will now derive the formula for the integrating factor. \n\nConsider the first-order linear equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to rewrite the left-hand side as the derivative of a product.\n\nTo do that, we multiply both sides of the equation by some function $I(x)$ that we wish to determine.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I(x)P(x)}y} = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\nWe want the left-hand side to match the derivative of $y\\cdot I(x)$. But by the product rule,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr)\n=\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I'(x)}y}.\n$$\n\nSo, if we choose $I(x)$ so that\n\n$$\nI'(x) = I(x)P(x),\n$$\n\nthen the left-hand side becomes $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr).$\n\nDividing both sides of $I'(x) = I(x)P(x)$ by $I(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{I'(x)}{I(x)} = P(x).\n$$\n\nNext, integrating both sides with respect to $x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{I}^{′}(x)}{I(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(I(x)))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(I(x)) & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{C}\\cdot {e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, because multiplying $I(x)$ by a nonzero constant does not change the method, we can choose $e^C = 1$ and define an integrating factor as\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a7f8aa2e-e022-57eb-be82-5f539a4512f6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14640 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Integrating Factor"} :step/id #uuid "ad072f0e-cc4b-5f68-b499-63509c901c8a" :step/math-academy-id 597611}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087942}]}
